# Large-disc filesystem regression test

Use a disposable, freshly initialised ADFS drive 5 with 1,056,964,608 bytes
(2048 cylinders, 16 heads, 63 sectors, 512-byte sectors). BigFS creates or
replaces `ADFS::5.$.BigFile`, reserving 600 MB. The raw sector test used during
bring-up must run before formatting; it is not part of this file test.

Load the plain BASIC source with `TEXTLOAD`, then `RUN`. The test checks the
disc size, creates a 600 MB file, writes four consecutive 4 KB patterns
starting 1 KB below the 512 MB file offset, closes and reopens the file,
checks its extent, seeks back and compares every word. It reports BIGFS PASS
or the failing line. The large file remains available for dismount/remount
and deletion checks. Delete BigFile afterwards to return its allocation.

This test addresses the 512 MB boundary. It does not verify the 2 GB per-file
limit, large-disc booting, LBA, other controllers, or physical hardware.

## Raw driver test

`BigIDE.s` is a GNU assembler source for an ARM3 diagnostic module. It writes
2 KB blocks at sectors 16, 1048574, 1048576, 1048578 and 2064380 on drive 5,
then clears the buffer and compares readback byte by byte. Each address is
tested with buffer alignments 0, 1, 2 and 3. The block starting at sector 1048574 crosses
512 MB and the final block ends at the disc boundary. These writes destroy
any existing filesystem content at those locations: use a separate blank
image of the geometry above, before formatting it.

Build on the host:

```
arm-none-eabi-as -o BigIDE.o BigIDE.s
arm-none-eabi-objcopy -O binary BigIDE.o BigIDE,ffa
```

Configure two IDE drives and load the module with `RMLoad`. It prints PASS
or FAIL. Its BIGIDE01 result block holds the completed comparison count
(20 on success), status (&600D on success), failing sector and buffer address.
The 8 KB buffer remains claimed for inspection; reset the guest afterwards.
Host-side inspection of the sparse image should confirm that the written
patterns occur at the intended byte offsets, excluding address wraparound.
